// Production case study: VGA (640x480) video frame-strip 3x3 box-blur stencil.
//
// This file is the PRODUCTION WITNESS for TASK-0455.03. It is a
// realistic-size instance of the same 3x3 box blur as the toy example
// 05-stencil (16x16), scaled to a STRIP OF 32 VGA FRAMES so that
// runtime dominates the process-startup floor.
//
// SIZE JUSTIFICATION (the load-bearing decision; see docs/case-study.md)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// A SINGLE VGA frame (640x480, 307200 px) blurs in ~4.5ms wall on the
// dev machine — but the measured nucleus-generated STARTUP FLOOR (spawn
// + runtime init + trivial IO) is ~0.6ms, so a single frame clears the
// floor by only ~8x, far short of the >=100x the case study must show.
// The blur compute is sub-millisecond at VGA; the 4.5ms is dominated by
// process spawn and 2.4MB of IO. To make COMPUTE dominate, the problem
// must be ~25x-40x larger.
//
// The realistic production unit for an image pipeline is a STREAM of
// frames, not one frame. We model that as a 32-frame strip stacked
// vertically: H = 32 * 480 = 15360, W = 640. This is ~half a second of
// 60fps VGA video processed as one batch — 9.83M px, 39MB per array.
// Measured: see docs/case-study.md sec 5 for the authoritative
// numbers (the doc, not this header, is the single source of truth —
// an earlier copy here drifted from the doc within one wave).
//
// Stacking frames into one tall image keeps the net LOOP-DEPTH-0 (a
// single-shot blur over a bigger array) — which is exactly why the
// landed symbolic gate carries it FLAT in N (see below). A frame LOOP
// (for f in 0..32) would instead put the cross-worker transfer inside a
// Repeat, expanding the net per iteration — the shape the gate CANNOT
// carry at realistic counts (16-jacobi class). The strip framing is the
// honest way to scale a single-pass stencil; the cross-frame seam rows
// blur across the 480-row boundary, a documented, benign artifact (the
// reference oracle blurs the identical strip, so byte-identity holds).
//
// It lives under docs/case-study/ (NOT under nuc-nucleus/examples/) so
// the e2e matrix — which enumerates only nuc-nucleus/examples/<NN-name>/
// — does NOT pick it up. The case study is exercised by its own
// `just case-study` recipe instead (scripts/case-study-run.sh).
//
// WHY THIS WORKLOAD (honesty about what the landed machinery carries)
// -------------------------------------------------------------------
// A single-pass stencil's distributed (partition=rows) net is
// LOOP-DEPTH-0: one Push and one Wait per shared buffer place, fired
// once, NOT inside an iteration Repeat. That is exactly the subclass
// the symbolic soundness gate (TASK-0455.01, landed) proves bounded +
// deadlock-free FLAT IN N — the gate's peak-occupancy argument runs on
// the rolled ACFG and never expands the net. So this workload compiles
// at VGA size in milliseconds with a few MB of gate RSS, where the
// expanded-replay gate would have demanded roughly 4-12GB (19.6M
// nodes x ~190-930B/node; consistent with docs/case-study.md sec 4).
//
// What the landed machinery does NOT carry at realistic N, and which
// is therefore deliberately NOT this case study:
//   - 16-jacobi (loop-carried / iterative stencil): each time-step
//     Repeat carries a cross-worker halo transfer, so the net expands
//     per iteration — still gated by the expanded replay. Realistic
//     iteration counts OOM. See sec:fw-quant.
//   - 09-producer-consumer / 13-cnn (pipelined): pre-marked buffers
//     inside a Repeat — also fall back to expansion.
// Picking the single-pass stencil is an HONEST choice of a workload
// the gate actually carries, stated as such (TASK-0455.01 final summary).
//
// NUMERIC CHOICE: i32 with wrapping_add + truncating integer division,
// identical to 05-stencil. Integer arithmetic is bit-deterministic by
// language definition (PRD §10.1), which the cross-backend bit-identical
// differential and the independent reference oracle both require.

const H : usize = 15360;    // 32 VGA frames stacked (32 * 480); 15360 * 640 * 4 = 39_321_600 bytes
const W : usize = 640;      // image width (VGA)

// Whole-program input/output arrays. Single-assignment per PRD §6.2.1;
// the dataflow writes each interior img_out[y][x] exactly once. Each
// array is 15360*640 = 9_830_400 i32 = 39_321_600 bytes (~39 MB).
data img_in  : i32[H][W];
data img_out : i32[H][W];

// Pure compute kernel: reorderable, deduplicable, eliminable. The
// scalar nine-argument signature matches the 3x3 access pattern below
// without aggregate-type machinery (same shape as 05-stencil's blur3).
kernel blur3 : (i32, i32, i32, i32, i32, i32, i32, i32, i32) -> i32 pure;

// I/O kernels. Effectful: ordering preserved within a basic block,
// never duplicated. Aggregate-typed in Nuc; Rust side uses Vec<i32>.
kernel load_image : ()          -> i32[H][W] effectful;
kernel save_image : (i32[H][W]) -> ()         effectful;

// Dataflow. The for-loop iteration variables (y, x) are the handles a
// schedule references. blur3's nine-neighbour access on img_in is what
// the compiler reads to infer the halo region (offsets -1,0,+1 on each
// axis) when a schedule distributes the work across row bands.
//
// Boundary handling: the loop walks y in 1..H-1 and x in 1..W-1 — the
// outermost ring of pixels is NOT written and stays at its
// single-assignment default (0). Identical policy to 05-stencil; the
// reference oracle (docs/case-study/reference/) matches it exactly.
// (The 31 inter-frame seam rows are blurred like any interior row — a
// benign artifact the reference reproduces, so byte-identity holds.)

img_in <-- load_image();

for y : 1 .. H-1 {
for x : 1 .. W-1 {
    img_out[y][x] <-- blur3(
        img_in[y-1][x-1], img_in[y-1][x], img_in[y-1][x+1],
        img_in[y  ][x-1], img_in[y  ][x], img_in[y  ][x+1],
        img_in[y+1][x-1], img_in[y+1][x], img_in[y+1][x+1]
    );
}}

save_image(img_out);
